That is most of the story, and the order matters: write the database first, then invalidate. Invalidate first and a concurrent read can slip in between, reload the old value, and re-cache it indefinitely. Deleting beats updating the cache entry in place, because delete has one outcome while a cache-side update duplicates the write logic and can diverge from it. The parts beyond 'just delete': the read path must treat a miss as normal and repopulate (cache-aside), every writer of that entity must go through the invalidating function — one forgotten writer resurrects the staleness — and in a multi-process deployment each process has its own cache, so invalidation has to cross processes via a shared cache or a pub/sub message. Expiry (TTL) then becomes a backstop for the invalidations you missed, not the primary mechanism.

