This is copy-on-write: the update path pays for a full copy and a single atomic reference swap, and in exchange every reader holding the old reference owns a consistent snapshot forever. It works because the swap itself is one assignment — in CPython a single reference assignment is atomic — so there is no moment when the store is half-built. The trade-offs are honest ones: updates cost O(size of state), and writers must be serialised among themselves (two concurrent copy-modify-swaps can still lose one update, since both start from the same base). It shines for read-heavy, write-rare state like configuration and feature flags, and it is the same idea behind immutable data structures and MVCC databases: never edit the past, publish a new version.

