Every persisted payload carries a version, and loading is a staircase: small pure functions that lift version N to N+1 (rename `color` to `theme` and add the new field's default), applied in order until the payload is current. Each step is tiny, testable, and only knows about its two neighbouring versions, so a three-year-old file migrates through every intermediate shape. The rules that keep this sane: never edit a shipped migration — add the next step instead; migrate on load and keep the in-memory model always at the current version, so application code never branches on age; and keep the defaults for new fields inside the migration, not scattered through the app. The alternative — one-off `getattr` fallbacks at every use site — smears version knowledge across the codebase and never gets cleaned up. Note this is also why many teams prefer JSON or another explicit format over pickle for state that must outlive the code: the format stays readable when the classes are gone.

