Your first version presumably did `DEFAULTS.update(overrides)` and returned DEFAULTS itself, so one user's beta flag became everyone's — the defaults stopped being defaults. The layered version treats each layer as immutable input and builds a fresh merged view per query: copy the base, apply overrides onto the copy, return the copy. Precedence is just the order of updates — defaults, then tenant, then user — and because nothing shared is mutated, there is no leak and no need to 'undo' a user's flags later. Two refinements for real systems: freeze the defaults (a MappingProxyType or a module constant you never write) so an accidental mutation raises instead of leaking, and decide explicitly whether removing an override should reveal the default again — with the merge-on-read design it does so automatically, which is usually the behaviour you want.

