This is the lost-update problem wearing a workflow costume. Each thread snapshot the whole dict, modified one field of its private copy, and wrote the copy back; the second write restored the first thread's field to the stale value it had read. No amount of care with timing fixes it — the unit of writing is wrong. The repairs, by granularity: update individual fields in the shared structure (shown), so writers touch disjoint keys; hold a lock around read-modify-write when fields are interdependent; or give each step ownership of its own status record and have readers aggregate, removing the shared dict entirely. In a database the equivalents are column-level UPDATE statements instead of writing back a whole ORM object loaded earlier — the 'detached entity overwrite' bug that this miniature reproduces. Whenever you see state written as whole blobs by multiple writers, expect this failure mode.

