Your doubled aggregates come from state whose lifetime is the process, serving a computation whose natural lifetime is the run. Moving the accumulator inside the run — created empty at the start, returned as a result, garbage after — makes the pipeline a pure function of its input: same input, same output, rerun as often as you like. That property is worth more than idempotency; it makes the job resumable and testable. When a batch genuinely must accumulate across runs (a daily rollup), the accumulation point becomes an explicit store with explicit semantics: keyed by run id or date partition, written transactionally at the end, so a rerun overwrites its own partition instead of adding to it. The pattern to internalise: for every piece of mutable state, be able to say when it is born, who owns it, and when it dies. 'It lives in a global and accumulates' is the absence of an answer, and pipelines find that absence at 3 a.m.

