The double recompute and the stale intermediate come from propagating eagerly along edges: A notifies B and C; B's update immediately triggers D while C is still stale, then C's update triggers D again. Topological recomputation fixes both: collect the affected subgraph, order it so every node is evaluated after all of its dependencies, and evaluate each exactly once against fully-fresh inputs — D sees the new B and the new C together, so the inconsistent intermediate value never exists, even transiently. This is the algorithm inside spreadsheets, build systems, and reactive frameworks that batch updates. Two refinements matter at scale: restrict the pass to nodes actually downstream of the change (the trace recomputes the whole small graph), and detect cycles during the ordering — a dependency cycle has no topological order, and the system must reject it at edit time rather than loop forever at recompute time. Glitches are not cosmetic: any observer reading mid-propagation — a renderer, a save routine — can persist the impossible value.

