recovered from clean log: {'a': 1, 'b': 2, 'c': 3}

The design assumption is that a crash can interrupt a write at any byte, so the log must be self-validating at record granularity. Framing each record with its length and a CRC lets recovery verify records one by one and stop at the first that fails: everything before is committed history, the torn tail is discarded as never having happened, and the database opens. The subtlety is the ordering guarantee that makes this sound — a record is only acknowledged to the caller after it is fully written and flushed (fsync) — so discarding the tail never discards an acknowledged write. Group commit, double-write buffers, and page checksums in real databases are all refinements of this same contract: the durable prefix is truth, the tail is suspect, and recovery is a fold over the valid prefix. Your loader's mistake was treating a parse error as fatal corruption of the whole log rather than as the expected signature of a crash.

